window.portfolioProjects=[
  {id:'aster',name:'Aster',category:'Product design & development',year:'2026',role:'Product thinking, interface design, frontend prototype',stack:'React / TypeScript concept',image:'aster.svg',summary:'A calmer corner for work that matters.',challenge:'Busy task tools often turn planning into another job. This self-directed concept asks what a small team could do with fewer surfaces and a more deliberate view of the day.',approach:'Start with one short list, keep completed work visible, and make the next action obvious. The prototype uses labelled native checkboxes, clear progress, and a hard limit that keeps the exercise small.',learning:'Restraint is a feature. A useful empty state and predictable keyboard behavior matter more than another dashboard tile.',deliverables:['A focused daily list and readable progress','Add, complete, and remove real in-memory tasks','Reset on close; no accounts or cloud synchronization'],demo:'tasks'},
  {id:'fieldwork',name:'Fieldwork',category:'Editorial website',year:'2026',role:'Art direction, typography, responsive web',stack:'Next.js / responsive typography concept',image:'fieldwork.svg',summary:'A little room for ideas to grow.',challenge:'A small independent journal needs to make space for the writing rather than compete with it. The concept pairs a quiet editorial voice with a simple path from a topic to a complete short read.',approach:'Use strong typography, honest reading times, and a single topic filter. The local prototype contains four original micro-essays, so every article opens actual writing rather than an empty destination.',learning:'Readable measure and a useful index can do more for discovery than an elaborate content interface.',deliverables:['Four original, complete micro-essays','Topic filtering and local article reader','Keyboard-accessible navigation without a CMS'],demo:'journal'},
  {id:'relay',name:'Relay',category:'Data & interface design',year:'2025',role:'Information design, data visualization, frontend prototype',stack:'React / accessible data visualization concept',image:'relay.svg',summary:'Less noise. A clearer picture.',challenge:'A chart should answer a question before it becomes decoration. Relay explores a compact view of a fictional small product, with raw counts close to the visual summary.',approach:'Keep one metric per chart, pair each bar with its exact value, and provide the same data in a semantic table. Switching quarters changes all totals and the written comparison from the same local dataset.',learning:'A plain-language summary and a table make a chart more useful to everyone, not only people who cannot see it.',deliverables:['Three quarters of consistent fictional data','Live quarter switch, totals, and comparisons','Accessible chart plus exact-value table'],demo:'analytics'},
  {id:'goodform',name:'Goodform',category:'Open-source concept',year:'2025',role:'Design tokens, semantic components, interaction design',stack:'HTML / CSS / JavaScript reference',image:'goodform.svg',summary:'The small things, thoughtfully made.',challenge:'The smallest interface parts are used the most. Goodform is a tiny reference exercise in clear labels, helpful validation, and controls that behave the way people expect.',approach:'Use native form elements before adding abstraction. The demo validates an email address and a choice of update topics, then previews exactly what would be submitted without sending or storing it.',learning:'A compact component is only good if its labels, errors, focus behavior, and empty states have been considered too.',deliverables:['Labelled, keyboard-first form controls','Native validation and a text-safe result preview','Original downloadable standalone reference source'],demo:'form'}
];
window.portfolioJournal=[
  {id:'details',topic:'Design',title:'The quiet value of small details',minutes:2,text:'A good interface rarely asks for credit. It puts the label where you look first, keeps the important action within reach, and remembers that a person may be arriving with only a minute to spare.\n\nSmall details are not little decorations. A useful empty state tells you what to do next. A clear error explains how to recover. A visible focus ring makes the path through the page understandable without a mouse.\n\nThe work is not to make every detail remarkable. It is to make the whole experience feel less demanding. Start with one place where someone hesitates, make that place a little clearer, and test the difference with a real task.'},
  {id:'materials',topic:'Process',title:'Give an idea something to push against',minutes:2,text:'An idea can sound perfect while it is still a sentence. Make it tangible and the useful questions begin. What happens when the title is longer? Where does the focus go? Does the empty state still make sense?\n\nA small prototype is a conversation partner, not a promise of a finished product. Give it one real input, one complete outcome, and enough honest limitations to keep the feedback grounded.\n\nThen watch someone use it. Resist explaining the interface before they have a chance to meet it. The places where your explanation feels necessary are often the places worth changing next.'},
  {id:'labels',topic:'Development',title:'A label is part of the interface',minutes:1,text:'A placeholder disappears at exactly the moment a person begins their work. A label stays. That small difference helps a tired reader, a screen reader, and someone returning after a distraction.\n\nNative controls bring a long history of keyboard behavior with them. Use that history. Associate every label with its field, name errors clearly, and keep the correction near the thing that needs attention.\n\nAn accessible form is not a special version of a normal form. It is a normal form with fewer assumptions about the person using it.'},
  {id:'space',topic:'Design',title:'Leave a little room',minutes:1,text:'White space is not what remains when the content is finished. It is one of the ways a page explains itself. A little room can tell you that two ideas belong together, or that it is time to pause before the next one.\n\nStart with the reading order. Make the most important relationship visible, then remove the borders and boxes that are no longer doing a job.\n\nThe goal is not emptiness. It is a page where the content has enough room to become clear.'}
];
